1
00:00:00,720 --> 00:00:01,870
Here's something strange.

2
00:00:02,230 --> 00:00:08,598
A bridge can carry a line of heavy trucks without a wobble, and still be shaken by people simply walking across it.

3
00:00:09,058 --> 00:00:12,828
The reason is resonance, and once you see it, you'll notice it everywhere.

4
00:00:13,788 --> 00:00:15,508
Think about pushing a child on a swing.

5
00:00:15,868 --> 00:00:18,337
Push at random moments, and nothing much happens.

6
00:00:18,797 --> 00:00:23,478
But push once every swing, right at the top, and each small push adds to the last.

7
00:00:23,888 --> 00:00:27,517
The swing has its own rhythm, and matching that rhythm is the whole trick.

8
00:00:27,877 --> 00:00:29,994
That rhythm is called the natural frequency.

9
00:00:30,954 --> 00:00:35,543
Everything has one: a guitar string, a wine glass, a skyscraper, a bridge.

10
00:00:35,953 --> 00:00:41,345
A tall building might sway once every few seconds, and a footbridge might bounce about twice a second.

11
00:00:41,755 --> 00:00:46,541
When a force keeps arriving at that same rhythm, small pushes stack up into big motion.

12
00:00:47,501 --> 00:00:54,530
In June 2000, London opened the Millennium Bridge, and on opening day it began to sway sideways under the crowds.

13
00:00:54,940 --> 00:00:59,339
When we walk, our bodies rock slightly from side to side, about once a second.

14
00:00:59,749 --> 00:01:05,892
As the deck started to move, people adjusted their steps to keep their balance, and that put them in step with the bridge.

15
00:01:06,302 --> 00:01:11,506
Thousands of small sideways pushes, all in time. The bridge closed after just two days.

16
00:01:12,466 --> 00:01:20,349
The fix wasn't to make it stronger. Engineers added dampers, devices that soak up motion the way shock absorbers do in a car.

17
00:01:20,759 --> 00:01:27,990
First they measured the motion, then they fitted 37 fluid dampers, and finally they tested it with crowds of volunteers.

18
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:32,248
It reopened in 2002, and it has stayed steady ever since.

19
00:01:33,208 --> 00:01:41,296
Tall buildings use a similar idea. Taipei 101 hangs a steel ball weighing about 660 tonnes near its top.

20
00:01:41,706 --> 00:01:46,264
When the wind pushes the tower one way, the heavy ball lags behind and pulls it back.

21
00:01:47,224 --> 00:01:52,360
So, resonance in one line: small pushes, perfectly timed, can beat brute force.

22
00:01:52,820 --> 00:01:57,006
Engineers don't fight it with strength. They change the rhythm, or they soak it up.

